title: Episode 11 | Doing More with Less
narrator: bf_emma en-gb
cast: ada=engineer:af_heart, ben=worker:am_michael, cy=boss:bm_george:en-gb
---
@zone bench
N: The connected firmware is finished. It has Wi-Fi, encryption, signed updates, a display, and a history log. There is just one problem.
ben!: The linker says the program is nine kilobytes too big for the flash. And RAM is over by six hundred bytes.
cy: So buy a bigger chip.
ada: The boards are already designed, and five thousand chips are already ordered. We are not buying a bigger chip. We are going to make this program smaller, and faster while we are at it.
N: This is a classic embedded moment. The hardware is fixed. The features are promised. And the only way forward is to understand exactly where every byte and every microsecond goes.
# Chapter One | Reading the Map File
@zone screen
N: Step one is not guessing. It is measuring. And the tool for measuring memory is the map file the linker writes every time it builds.
%table From the map file
- Section | Size | Lives in
- .text (code) | 54.2 KB | flash
- .rodata (constants) | 13.1 KB | flash
- .data (initialized vars) | 0.4 KB | flash + RAM
- .bss (zeroed vars) | 5.9 KB | RAM
- stack | 2.0 KB | RAM
ada: Code goes in the text section. Constant data, like strings and tables, in read-only data. Initialized variables need space twice: their starting values in flash, and the variables themselves in RAM. Zero-initialized variables only need RAM.
N: The map file also lists every function and variable with its size. Sort that list, and the biggest offenders jump out.
%chart Biggest code users in KB
- bar Size: 11.8,7.2,6.5,4.1,3.0,2.2
xl TLS crypto,printf,font data,Wi-Fi driver,JSON,float math
ben?: Printf? Seven kilobytes for printing text?
ada: A full printf handles every format, including floating point. We barely use it. That is our first target.
# Chapter Two | Shrinking the Code
@zone screen
%list Ways to shrink code
- Turn on size optimization
- Use a smaller printf, or none at all
- Avoid pulling in float libraries
- Remove dead code and debug features
- Share common code instead of copying
- Move data tables into compact formats
ada: First, the compiler. Optimizing for size instead of speed often saves ten to twenty percent immediately. Combined with link-time optimization, the linker can also discard functions nobody calls.
N: Second, libraries. A single call to a floating point function can drag in kilobytes of software math routines on a chip without a floating point unit. Replacing a few float calculations with integer math removed six kilobytes by itself.
%code Before and after
| // before: drags in float formatting and math
| printf("Moisture: %.1f%%\\n", moisture_f);
|
| // after: integer tenths, tiny formatter
|>log_u32x10("Moisture", moisture_tenths);
ben: And the font. We had three sizes of a font that includes every accented character. Sprout only displays digits and a few words. Cutting the font to the characters we use saved five kilobytes.
N: Each change is small. Together they save fourteen kilobytes, and the program fits with room to spare. The key was the map file telling the team where to look, instead of shaving random functions.
cy^: So the program was full of things nobody used? Like my garage.
ada~: Exactly like your garage. And just like a garage, it fills up again unless someone checks. So we add a size report to every build.
N: A build that prints the flash and RAM totals, and fails if they grow past a limit, turns memory from a surprise into a number the whole team watches. When a change adds two kilobytes, it is noticed that day, not three months later.
%chart Flash use per release in KB
- line Flash used: 41,48,55,63,73,59
- line Limit: 64,64,64,64,64,64
xl v0.1,v0.2,v0.3,v0.4,v0.5,v0.6
ben: Look at that. We crossed the line at version zero point five and nobody noticed until the linker complained. With a report on every build, we would have seen it creeping up for weeks.
# Chapter Three | Shrinking RAM Use
@zone screen
N: RAM is often tighter than flash, and harder to measure, because the stack and the heap change while the program runs.
%memory Where RAM went
- 0x2000_2000 | Stack | 2 | measured peak 1.1 KB
- 0x2000_1800 | Heap | 1 | for TLS
- 0x2000_0800 | Buffers | 2.5 | display, network
- 0x2000_0000 | Globals | 1.2 | state, settings
ada: Globals are visible in the map file. The stack is not. We measure it with the watermark trick from the bugs episode: fill it with a pattern at boot, run everything hard, then check how much pattern survived.
N: The stack had two kilobytes reserved, but the peak use was one point one. That spare can be reclaimed, carefully, leaving a safety margin for code paths the test might not have hit.
%list RAM savings
- Shrink the stack to measured peak plus margin
- Share one buffer between features that never run together
- Keep constant tables in flash, not RAM
- Use smaller types for small numbers
- Pack flags into bits
ben: Sharing buffers is a big one. The network receive buffer and the display frame buffer are never busy at the same time, because we turn the display off during uploads. So they can use the same memory.
ada: That kind of sharing must be documented loudly. Otherwise, next year someone adds a progress bar during uploads, and the display scribbles over network data.
N: One more RAM trap is the humble constant table. In C, a table declared without const may be copied into RAM at startup, even though it never changes. Marking it const lets the linker leave it in flash, where it belongs.
%code Keep constant data in flash
| // copied into RAM at boot: 512 bytes wasted
| uint16_t moisture_curve[256] = { ... };
|
|>// stays in flash: zero RAM
| const uint16_t moisture_curve[256] = { ... };
ada: One keyword, five hundred bytes of RAM back. That and the stack trim got us under the RAM limit, with three hundred bytes to spare.
cy?: Three hundred bytes. Is that a lot?
ada: It is enough for now. Tight, but honest.
# Chapter Four | The Trouble with Malloc
@zone screen
N: The cryptography library wants to allocate memory dynamically, with malloc. Many embedded teams avoid dynamic allocation entirely. Ada explains why.
%blocks Heap fragmentation
- Free 4 KB > Alloc 1 KB + 2 KB + 1 KB
- Alloc 1 KB + 2 KB + 1 KB > Free the two 1 KB blocks
- Free the two 1 KB blocks > Need 2 KB: fails!
- Need 2 KB: fails!
ada: Allocate and free blocks of different sizes over time, and free memory gets chopped into small, scattered pieces. There may be plenty free in total, but no single piece big enough. That is fragmentation.
N: On a desktop, a failed allocation is rare and the operating system has tricks. On a microcontroller running for months, a slowly fragmenting heap is a time bomb with a very long fuse.
%list Safer alternatives
- Allocate everything once at startup
- Use fixed-size memory pools
- Use static buffers sized for the worst case
- If you must use a heap, never free, or free in reverse order
ada: Our approach: allocate everything the crypto library needs at boot, once, and never free it. If it fits on day one, it fits on day five hundred.
ben: And memory pools for messages. Every message buffer is exactly the same size, so any free buffer fits any request. No fragmentation possible.
# Chapter Five | Finding Where Time Goes
@zone bench
N: Next problem. The watering screen feels sluggish. Pressing a key takes nearly half a second to update the display. Cy calls it, the sleepy keypad.
ada: Before optimizing anything, we profile. Guessing where time goes is wrong surprisingly often.
%list Profiling tools
- Toggle a pin, measure with a scope
- Read a cycle counter before and after
- Sample the program counter periodically
- Count how often functions run
ada>: The simplest method is the debug pin: set it high at the start of a function, low at the end, and look at the scope. The width of the pulse is the time.
N: Many processors also have a cycle counter, a register that counts every clock cycle. Reading it before and after a block of code gives a precise duration, without a scope.
%code Timing with the cycle counter
| uint32_t start = CYCLE_COUNTER;
| display_redraw();
| uint32_t cycles = CYCLE_COUNTER - start;
|>log_u32("redraw cycles", cycles);     // 3,200,000 at 8 MHz = 0.4 s
ben!: Point four seconds to redraw the screen? That is the whole delay!
N: A third method, statistical profiling, interrupts the program thousands of times per second and records where the program counter is. Functions that appear in many samples are where time is spent.
# Chapter Six | Optimizing the Hot Spot
@zone screen
N: The redraw calls draw pixel one point one million times per screen. Each call computes the pixel's address, checks bounds, and sets one bit.
%code The slow inner loop
| for (int y = 0; y < 64; y++)
|   for (int x = 0; x < 128; x++)
|>    draw_pixel(x, y, color_at(x, y));   // function call per pixel
ada: The fix is not to make draw pixel faster. It is to stop calling it so often. Most of the screen is unchanged between frames. And whole bytes can be written at once instead of single bits.
%list Optimization, in order of payoff
- Do less: skip work that is not needed
- Choose a better algorithm
- Move work out of inner loops
- Use the hardware: DMA, bigger transfers
- Only then: micro-tune the code
ada>: First, redraw only regions that changed. Second, write eight pixels per byte instead of one. Third, send rows using DMA instead of the processor.
%chart Redraw time in milliseconds
- bar Time: 400,95,22,6
xl original,dirty regions,byte writes,DMA
N: From four hundred milliseconds to six. The keypad wakes up. And notice: not a single trick involved clever assembly. Doing less and using the hardware beat micro-optimization by a mile.
cy^: Sixty-six times faster! I am putting that in the next pitch deck.
ben~: You put the six microseconds in the last pitch deck.
N: There is a balance between speed and size. Techniques that make code faster, like unrolling loops or inlining functions, often make it bigger. Techniques that make it smaller can make it slower.
%table Speed versus size
- Technique | Speed | Size
- Inline small functions | faster | bigger
- Unroll loops | faster | bigger
- Lookup tables | faster | bigger
- Optimize for size | slower | smaller
ada: The trick is to apply speed optimizations only to the few functions that the profile says are hot, and optimize everything else for size. Most code runs rarely. It should be small. A tiny fraction runs constantly. It should be fast.
# Chapter Seven | Small Tricks That Help
@zone screen
N: Once the big wins are taken, a few smaller habits still help in hot code.
%list Small habits for hot code
- Use the processor's natural word size
- Avoid division in loops; precompute
- Keep data together in memory
- Use lookup tables for repeated math
- Read the compiler's output for the hottest loop
ada: Using the natural word size matters on thirty-two bit processors. An eight bit loop counter can force extra instructions to wrap it at two hundred fifty-five, while a thirty-two bit one is free.
N: And sometimes, look at the assembly. For the one loop that dominates a profile, reading what the compiler produced is the only way to know whether the code is doing what you think.
ada: But measure after every change. Optimizations that should help sometimes make things slower, and the only judge is the cycle counter.
# Recap | Measure, Then Cut
@zone green
%list Today we learned
- The map file shows where flash and RAM go
- Size optimization, lean libraries, trimmed data
- Measure stack peaks; share buffers carefully
- Avoid malloc fragmentation; use pools
- Profile with pins, cycle counters, sampling
- Do less first; micro-tune last
N: To recap. When memory runs out, read the map file, sort by size, and attack the biggest items: oversized libraries, float math, fonts and tables.
N: RAM needs runtime measurement: stack watermarks, shared buffers, and allocation at startup or from fixed pools instead of a fragmenting heap.
N: For speed, profile first. Then do less, choose better algorithms, and use hardware like DMA, before reaching for micro-optimizations.
cy: Fits in flash, fits in RAM, keypad is fast. Can we ship?
ada: Almost. Next episode, some of our math is wrong, slow, or both. Time to talk about numbers.
